WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.939
Film Courage: The question is, what stands
in most people’s way of achieving their

00:00:03.939 --> 00:00:08.260
dreams? It sounds simplistic…we can achieve
our dreams and there are a million books the

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.690
subject. What do you think is actually the
block for most people?

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:17.020
Dr. Ken Atchity: I’ve written a book called
Quit Your Day Job and Lead the Life of Your

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:21.300
Dreams based on my own experience and that
of others.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.449
One of my favorite stories…I was on Dr.
Joyce Brothers television show years ago with

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:30.460
a couple of other people and one of them who
was a man who was then in his 80’s and had

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:38.800
just received his law degree from The University
of Chicago and he told her that he was standing

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:45.150
in line for registration four years earlier
and one of the young people in line behind

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:50.190
him said “Sir, are you sure you’re in
the right line?” And he said “And I turned

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.770
around and I said what line should I be in?”

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:57.600
And I thought that is America. That’s the
essence of America, you are in whatever line

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:05.770
you want to be in this country and he fearlessly
walked up and stood in the line and got his

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:12.021
law degree at the age of 86 or whatever he
was and to me what stands in people’s way

00:01:12.021 --> 00:01:17.490
is fear and their friends inflict it on them.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:22.450
So one of the chapters in my book has to do
with distinguishing between friends and friendly

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:29.789
associates because when I left the academic
world I had a few friends &nbsp; and I had lots

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:35.119
of friendly associates and I learned the difference
when I decided to leave because I retained

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:41.029
a few friends but most everybody I did not
retain as friends because they thought I was

00:01:41.029 --> 00:01:47.819
absolutely crazy. They either thought that
that in kind of a benign way or they were

00:01:47.819 --> 00:01:53.509
just extremely angry that I was leaving a
tenured position.

00:01:53.509 --> 00:02:00.599
They thought that was completely ungrateful
and crazy. I can also say that they were fearful

00:02:00.599 --> 00:02:07.020
about it and I knew them well enough to know
that they were envious, they wished they could

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:12.040
do it but they wouldn’t do it because they
were set in their ways and that’s one of

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:16.739
the reasons I didn’t like tenure because
once you had tenure you didn’t have to publish

00:02:16.739 --> 00:02:22.410
anymore, you didn’t have to do anything
anymore. And of course if you were truly motivated

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:27.760
that’s not going to stop you and there were
a few people who were unstoppable. But mostly

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:34.180
they weren’t unstoppable and they just stopped
and to me that was a crime because I didn’t

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:40.319
understand anything other than the merit system
as something that should rule an academy of

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:41.540
ideas.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:47.359
So I think what makes people afraid is the
fear of being out on the street. You know

00:02:47.359 --> 00:02:54.329
it’s an image that I’ve had in the first
10 years of getting into a new world where

00:02:54.329 --> 00:02:58.890
I realized I wasn’t going to get a paycheck
every two weeks (regularly).

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:04.950
You have that image if you were raised by
Depression-era parents. And you also have

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:10.980
the other image of wolves at the door. I remember
that one because I found a quick way around

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:16.220
it. Go to the door, open the door and if you
don’t see any wolves at the door then there

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:22.409
are no wolves at the door. But it is an image
that pops into your mind in the middle of

00:03:22.409 --> 00:03:28.010
the night as does the homeless image and many
other things. But if you’re afraid of images

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:29.900
then you shouldn’t be in the world of images.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:36.489
I mean that’s what I do, I create images
and develop images and turn them into movies

00:03:36.489 --> 00:03:44.749
so how can I let images that are in my brain
control my actions. You have to learn to overcome

00:03:44.749 --> 00:03:54.340
that. So I think people have to clearly understand
themselves and decide on who to listen to.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:59.230
If you truly are a friend and you love somebody
you encourage them to fulfill their dreams

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:03.700
and I always did that to my students. I always
felt like you have a dream and you are afraid

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:09.110
of accomplishing it. What if your dream is
the most important dream that ever came along

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:13.529
in the human race and you don’t do anything
about it. It’s your dream and you do nothing

00:04:13.529 --> 00:04:20.630
about it. That’s a sacrilege. You had the
dream for a reason, it’s in your mind for

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:26.789
a reason. Either God put it there or it was
borne in your mind from some other source,

00:04:26.789 --> 00:04:31.259
why are you going to do something about it?
“Well because I’m afraid that my father

00:04:31.259 --> 00:04:35.250
and mother would be really upset.” And so
I go “Well this is a hypothetical fear about

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:43.620
something that hasn’t happened yet?” “Yes.”
“Then why not just do it and deal with the

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.419
possibility that this may never happen at
all?”

00:04:47.419 --> 00:04:53.220
It’s a matter of knowing yourself. That’s
one of the things I used to talk about first

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:57.940
in the book. I was raised on Greek philosophy
and when it said over the Oracle of Delphi

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:03.520
was “Know Thyself.” That was the most
important piece of knowledge that Plato and

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:10.360
Aristotle and Socrates taught and knowing
yourself means know you are going to be haunted

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.819
by this dream if you don’t do it.

00:05:12.819 --> 00:05:18.310
I mean I had a partner who said once when
her movie was in trouble “Maybe this is

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:22.730
one of those dreams that should never have
happened?” And I said “That is complete

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:31.860
blasphemy! You say that now but later you
will see that it was some other voice talking

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:37.550
to you other than your own voice because you
made this thing happen and you will be proud

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:40.130
of that.”

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:48.199
And simply fear is the number one impediment
for people going for their dreams and it’s

00:05:48.199 --> 00:05:54.070
fear and everyone knows the acronyms about
fear is things that haven’t happened yet,

00:05:54.070 --> 00:06:00.599
that may never happen (just like worry). And
we all do it, we all have fears, we all have

00:06:00.599 --> 00:06:08.819
worries. But overcoming your fear is what
valiant people do. It’s what people that

00:06:08.819 --> 00:06:15.720
you would like to be like do so why not do
it yourself and not have to live with the

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:21.509
regret which is the big monster equal to fear
that you have to live with if you are sitting

00:06:21.509 --> 00:06:27.419
on that proverbial front porch in your rocking
chair thinking about the dreams that you had

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:32.300
a didn’t do. To me that’s a terrible waste
of life to have that happen.

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:37.479
Film Courage: Also, stripping away illusions.
Can you talk about knowing thyself and being

00:06:37.479 --> 00:06:42.000
comfortable enough to know that if you have
to stand by yourself for awhile because you’ve

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.819
lost the illusion of some of the friendships
or peer group that you thought was going to

00:06:47.819 --> 00:06:52.700
be there with you, if for whatever reason
socially they’ve gone the other way, know

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:54.180
that this is okay as well.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:07:00.789
Dr. Atchity: That’s a very good point because
I think as you get older you realize that

00:07:00.789 --> 00:07:06.700
you cannot govern your life by what other
people think. And I live on the 11th floor

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:11.949
and I look out on the millions of lights in
Los Angeles and it’s a great comfort to

00:07:11.949 --> 00:07:20.099
think that there are a few lights out there
that love me. There are maybe fewer that hate

00:07:20.099 --> 00:07:27.120
me but there are millions that have no idea
that I exist. That’s comfortable and that’s

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:32.819
kind of the cosmic view of life when you think
about it. You are just one tiny piece of the

00:07:32.819 --> 00:07:40.539
massive cosmos that is going about its massive
mechanism on its own without any need for

00:07:40.539 --> 00:07:48.599
you to consult with it and for you to be worried
about what some person somewhere else thinks

00:07:48.599 --> 00:07:55.319
about you is a complete waste of your energy
in every way. Your job is to do what your

00:07:55.319 --> 00:08:01.949
dreams tell you to do and to do it with all
of your might the way the cosmos does and

00:08:01.949 --> 00:08:05.690
what other people are saying and thinking
and doing first of all because most of them

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:09.930
are not spending any time thinking about you
at all and could care less what you do or

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:15.569
don’t do. Most of them are thinking only
about themselves, so that’s a natural condition

00:08:15.569 --> 00:08:19.930
and why should you be any different? If you
have a dream, just do it.

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:25.469
And if you’re the crazy painter that has
been turning out paintings in the garage and

00:08:25.469 --> 00:08:29.170
everyone thinks you’re a crackpot but then
they learn that you sold one of your paintings

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:33.841
for a million dollars and now it’s going
to be in The Louvre, suddenly they go “I

00:08:33.841 --> 00:08:39.950
always knew that gal was a genius. She really
had talent from the very beginning.” People

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:44.810
change instantly (which shows how much value
their opinion really has).

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:52.110
That’s why I think you’ve got to really
listen to yourself and not listen to everybody

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:57.860
else. And you can tell your true friends because
the ones who support you in doing that are

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:04.840
your true friends and if the person who is
not supporting you…I’ve had several clients

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:12.730
in my career whose spouses did not support
them and my advice is divorce your spouse,

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:19.710
I’m sorry. I take this seriously. This is
a profession, this is a vocation. And if someone

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:26.820
close to you is tell you don’t do it, it’s
selfish, you’ve got to get someone else

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:29.320
close to you who will encourage you.

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:34.501
Because all of the monumental great things
in life are done by people who I think go

00:09:34.501 --> 00:09:40.760
for it and who are not afraid in taking a
chance and who are therefore support by a

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:48.150
few true friends or loved ones who tell them
to do it.

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:53.660
Many examples from my own life but when I
decided to leave the tenured position my daughter

00:09:53.660 --> 00:10:00.860
was a junior at Columbia University and one
thing that would be jeopardized was her senior

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:05.290
year at Columbia. And I brought her up to
Montreal where I was shooting a movie and

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:12.200
we had a long talk about it off set. And she
said “Dad you absolutely have to do this.

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:16.550
You have to do it. Don’t worry about that.”
And of course that problem got solved and

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:23.810
didn’t end up being a problem but it was
a concern but she had no concern for it and

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:29.780
that’s how I know who my true friends are.
And that’s how you would know too if you

00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:34.521
decide you want to do something. Listen carefully
to what the people around you say. Because

00:10:34.521 --> 00:10:39.780
when people are telling you no they are expressing
their own fears. And some of it may be goodhearted,

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:45.280
they are afraid that the things they fear
may happen to you. But if you’re willing

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:51.340
to take the risk, don’t let them influence
you because they’re not taking the risk

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:57.100
unless they depend on you and then you have
to figure that out.

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:02.030
I did certain things when I left that career
to make sure that those who depended on me

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:08.250
would not end up being left without resources
so I did what I had to do to make sure that

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:15.620
happened and then once I did that my conscience
was clear and I was able to embrace it fully

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:22.620
with all of the risks that it entailed. And
no regrets even though there were some very

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:31.300
dark times and there are always ups and downs
in a business like this one and in a career

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:36.830
that is bereft of security.

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:42.230
The other side of the coin is that as much
as security is an illusion, rejection is also

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:48.160
an illusion because you can take as many chances
as you want. I constantly hear people tell

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:52.660
me (even on the phone this morning) “You
only get one shot.” (That was a distributor

00:11:52.660 --> 00:11:58.600
telling me we only get one shot). And I thought
“Well okay, maybe that is true for you but

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:04.570
I get as many shots as I want to take.”
And Hollywood, first of all it doesn’t exist,

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:10.570
what is Hollywood? It’s just a concept.
But in reality the business that I’m in

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:15.670
all you have to do is tell somebody I’ve
got a great new story and they are all ears

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:20.220
immediately. They don’t care that it’s
been ten years since you’ve talked to them.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:24.860
You spend a a few seconds in chit chat and
then they want to hear the story.

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:31.520
So you can take as many chances as you want
to take unless your own psychology disallows

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:39.880
that because it wants you to get depressed
and spend…go into a coma of unhappiness

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:44.810
and take rejection seriously, etc. I just
don’t have time.

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:49.270
One of my essays is called The Waiting Room
and it’s about what you do while you are

00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:55.610
waiting for an answer on a creative project.
Well you don’t wait, you do something else.

00:12:55.610 --> 00:13:01.621
You make another creative project, you get
it going. And if you keep doing that, every

00:13:01.621 --> 00:13:06.080
project has it’s own clock, you can’t
do much to control that clock but you can

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:11.540
be doing another project. And soon enough
you have projects all around you that are

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:18.480
in various states of ripeness and they will
happen in their own time and your biggest

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:22.750
problem will be what if two of them happen
at the same time. I always say “Don’t

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:29.310
worry about that.” You don’t want to have
the problem of nothing happening.

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:34.510
So no, you don’t wait at all and I think
a lot of writers torture themselves because

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:41.100
they wait you know they send off a manuscript
hypothetically into snail mail. No one does

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:44.820
that anymore but they send it off and then
they wait for an answer. Why would they wait

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:49.750
for an answer? That’s a complete waste of
time. Instead you instantly work on something

00:13:49.750 --> 00:13:57.670
else and that way when something comes from
the first thing, you’re just surprised and

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:02.030
you deal with it immediately without wasting
any kind of psychological energy on it. You

00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:09.910
just…if it’s a rejection…you just take
it and you move on. And if it somebody offering

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:12.590
you a deal then you consider the deal.

00:14:12.590 --> 00:14:20.170
But writers feel like they have to spend an
additional 90% of their time fretting over

00:14:20.170 --> 00:14:26.210
it all, analyzing it, soul searching over
it and you do that when you’re younger and

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:30.410
it’s fine to do some of it because you may
get a lot of creativity out of it. But once

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:36.260
you’ve gone through it and tortured yourself
to your own satisfaction, you don’t have

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:40.190
to do that all of the time, you can just go
back to work.

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:44.440
Ray Bradbury used to say that “Get back
to work. It will get rid of all those moods

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:48.990
you’re having.” And I always thought that
was the most brilliant advice. Work is the

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:49.320
solution.

